' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' yaml/lib.mfb — the package's public API: `parse` and `parseAll`.
'
' Both take a String and produce `json::Json`. Neither touches the filesystem:
' read the text yourself (`fs::readText`) and hand it over, which is the same
' split `csv::parse` and `json::parse` use.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORT json
IMPORT collections
IMPORT strings

DOC
  PACKAGE
  DESC Read a JSON-compatible subset of YAML 1.2 into the `json::Json` value
  DESC tree.
  DESC
  DESC `yaml::parse` turns one YAML document into a `json::Json` value and
  DESC `yaml::parseAll` turns a multi-document stream into a
  DESC `List OF json::Json`. The result is an ordinary `json::Json` tree, so
  DESC `json::get`, `json::getOr` and `json::stringify` all work on it, and
  DESC anything that already consumes JSON -- an HTTP body, a config reader --
  DESC consumes YAML unchanged. Because the model is `json::Json`, an importer
  DESC writes `IMPORT json` alongside `IMPORT yaml` to name the type.
  DESC
  DESC The package owns no filesystem access, exactly as `csv::parse` and
  DESC `json::parse` own none: read the text and pass the `String`.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT yaml
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET text AS String = fs::readText("config.yaml")
      LET value AS json::Json = yaml::parse(text)
      io::print(json::stringify(value))
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
  DESC
  DESC What the reader accepts
  DESC
  DESC Block mappings and block sequences; flow mappings and flow sequences
  DESC (`{a: 1}`, `[1, 2]`), which makes every JSON document a valid input;
  DESC plain, single-quoted and double-quoted scalars, including multi-line
  DESC ones; literal (`|`) and folded (`>`) block scalars with indentation and
  DESC chomping indicators; comments; `%YAML 1.2`; the `---` and `...` document
  DESC markers; and anchors and aliases.
  DESC
  DESC Deliberate limits
  DESC
  DESC YAML models more than JSON does, so the constructs that cannot be
  DESC carried faithfully are refused rather than converted into something that
  DESC merely looks similar. Every one of these fails with an `Error`, and none
  DESC of them silently changes the data:
  INFO Mapping keys must be strings. A JSON object key is a string, so a plain
  INFO key the schema types as anything else -- `12:`, `true:`, `null:`, an
  INFO empty key -- is rejected with `errorCode::ErrUnsupported`. Quote it
  INFO (`"12":`) to make it a string.
  INFO
  INFO Duplicate keys are rejected with `errorCode::ErrAlreadyExists`, rather
  INFO than resolved last-wins. A duplicate key in a config file is a mistake
  INFO worth hearing about.
  INFO
  INFO Multiple documents require `yaml::parseAll`. `yaml::parse` reads a
  INFO stream holding exactly one document and fails with
  INFO `errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat` on zero or on two.
  INFO
  INFO Tags and merge keys are rejected with `errorCode::ErrUnsupported`. That
  INFO covers every `!` and `!!` tag, the `%TAG` directive, and `<<`. A tag
  INFO selects a type outside the schema, and a merge key builds a mapping out
  INFO of other mappings -- both change what the document means, so neither is
  INFO guessed at.
  INFO
  INFO Aliases are expanded, and cyclic aliases are rejected. An anchor is
  INFO registered only once its own node is complete, so `&a [*a]` fails with
  INFO `errorCode::ErrNotFound` -- a cycle has no `json::Json` representation
  INFO and `json::stringify` could not terminate on one.
  INFO
  INFO Scalars are typed by the YAML 1.2 Core Schema and nothing else. `true`
  INFO and `false` are booleans; `null`, `~` and an empty value are null;
  INFO `[-+]?[0-9]+`, `0x…` and `0o…` are integers; the usual decimal and
  INFO exponent forms are floats; everything else is a string. In particular
  INFO `yes`, `no`, `on`, `off`, `y` and `n` are STRINGS, because the schema
  INFO that types them as booleans is YAML 1.1, which this reader does not
  INFO implement -- and `%YAML 1.1` is refused rather than read under 1.2 rules.
  INFO
  INFO Values with no JSON equivalent are rejected, not converted. `.inf`,
  INFO `-.inf` and `.nan` are numbers YAML can write and JSON cannot, so they
  INFO fail with `errorCode::ErrUnsupported` instead of becoming a string, a
  INFO null, or a zero.
  SEC Parser depth, alias expansion, and total node count are all bounded, so
  SEC untrusted YAML cannot exhaust memory or the stack. A document may nest
  SEC 100 levels, perform 10000 alias expansions, and materialise 1000000
  SEC nodes; passing any of those fails with the package's own
  SEC `yaml::expansionLimitCode()` (93110001). The node budget counts what an
  SEC alias EXPANDS TO rather than the one line that writes it, which is what
  SEC bounds a "billion laughs" document -- a few hundred bytes of nested
  SEC aliases that would otherwise name an exponentially large tree.
  DESC
  DESC Numbers are `json::JsonNum`, which holds a `Float`, exactly as
  DESC `json::parse` produces. An integer with more digits than binary64 holds
  DESC is rounded, and a magnitude too large for `Float` fails with
  DESC `errorCode::ErrOverflow` -- the same two behaviours JSON has here.
  DESC
  DESC Errors
  DESC
  DESC `errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat` for malformed YAML,
  DESC `errorCode::ErrUnsupported` for well-formed YAML outside the subset,
  DESC `errorCode::ErrAlreadyExists` for a duplicate key,
  DESC `errorCode::ErrNotFound` for an alias naming no anchor,
  DESC `errorCode::ErrDepthExceeded` past 100 levels of nesting,
  DESC `errorCode::ErrInvalidSurrogate` for a `\u` escape encoding an unpaired
  DESC surrogate, `errorCode::ErrOverflow` for a number too large for `Float`,
  DESC and `yaml::expansionLimitCode()` (93110001) past the alias or node
  DESC budget. That last one is a generator-9 code, which is not globally
  DESC unique, so match it only around a call you already know is this
  DESC package's.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC parse
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Read one YAML document from `text` and return it as a `json::Json`
  DESC value.
  DESC
  DESC The input must hold exactly one document. Zero documents -- an empty
  DESC string, or nothing but comments -- and two or more both fail; use
  DESC `yaml::parseAll` for a stream. A single `---` start marker is fine, and
  DESC so is a trailing `...`.
  ARG text The YAML text to read. Reading a file is the caller's job: pass `fs::readText(path)`.
  RET The document's root node as a `json::Json` value. A document with an empty body is `json::JsonNull`.
  ERROR 77050003 The YAML is malformed, or the input holds zero or more than one document.
  ERROR 77050007 The document uses a construct outside the supported subset: a tag, a merge key, an explicit `?` key, a non-string mapping key, `%TAG`, a YAML version other than 1.2, or `.inf`/`.nan`.
  ERROR 77050005 A mapping has a duplicate key.
  ERROR 77050004 An alias names no anchor defined before it, which is also how a cyclic alias is reported.
  ERROR 77050024 The document nests more than 100 levels deep.
  ERROR 77050025 A `\u` escape in a double-quoted scalar encodes an unpaired surrogate.
  ERROR 77050010 A number in the document is too large for a `Float`.
  ERROR 93110001 The document expands past the alias-expansion or total-node limit.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT yaml
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET value AS json::Json = yaml::parse("name: mfb" & "\n" & "tags: [fast, small]" & "\n")
      io::print(json::stringify(value))
      ' {"name":"mfb","tags":["fast","small"]}
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC parse(text AS String) AS json::Json
  LET bytes AS List OF Byte = strings::toBytes(text)
  LET lines AS Lines = scanLines(bytes)
  LET starts AS List OF Integer = lines.starts
  LET ends AS List OF Integer = lines.ends
  LET indents AS List OF Integer = lines.indents
  LET documents AS Documents = splitDocuments(bytes, starts, ends, indents)
  LET count AS Integer = len(documents.bodyLines)
  IF count = 0 THEN
    FAIL error(ERR_FORMAT, "yaml: the input holds no document, and yaml::parse reads exactly one")
  END IF
  IF count > 1 THEN
    FAIL error(ERR_FORMAT, "yaml: the input holds " & toString(count) & " documents; yaml::parse reads exactly one -- use yaml::parseAll")
  END IF
  RETURN parseDocument(bytes, starts, ends, indents, collections::get(documents.bodyLines, 0), collections::get(documents.bodyCols, 0), collections::get(documents.endLines, 0))
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC parseAll
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Read every YAML document in `text` and return them in stream order.
  DESC
  DESC Documents are separated by a `---` start marker and may be closed by a
  DESC `...` end marker. An input holding no document at all -- empty, or
  DESC nothing but comments and blank lines -- yields an empty list rather than
  DESC failing; that is the one place this differs from `yaml::parse`, which
  DESC needs exactly one.
  ARG text The YAML text to read. Reading a file is the caller's job: pass `fs::readText(path)`.
  RET One `json::Json` value per document, in stream order. A document with an empty body is `json::JsonNull`.
  ERROR 77050003 The YAML is malformed.
  ERROR 77050007 A document uses a construct outside the supported subset: a tag, a merge key, an explicit `?` key, a non-string mapping key, `%TAG`, a YAML version other than 1.2, or `.inf`/`.nan`.
  ERROR 77050005 A mapping has a duplicate key.
  ERROR 77050004 An alias names no anchor defined before it, which is also how a cyclic alias is reported.
  ERROR 77050024 A document nests more than 100 levels deep.
  ERROR 77050025 A `\u` escape in a double-quoted scalar encodes an unpaired surrogate.
  ERROR 77050010 A number in a document is too large for a `Float`.
  ERROR 93110001 A document expands past the alias-expansion or total-node limit.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT yaml
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Integer
      LET stream AS String = "--- one" & "\n" & "--- two" & "\n"
      FOR EACH document IN yaml::parseAll(stream)
        io::print(json::stringify(document))
      NEXT
      ' "one"
      ' "two"
      RETURN 0
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC parseAll(text AS String) AS List OF json::Json
  LET bytes AS List OF Byte = strings::toBytes(text)
  LET lines AS Lines = scanLines(bytes)
  LET starts AS List OF Integer = lines.starts
  LET ends AS List OF Integer = lines.ends
  LET indents AS List OF Integer = lines.indents
  LET documents AS Documents = splitDocuments(bytes, starts, ends, indents)
  LET bodyLines AS List OF Integer = documents.bodyLines
  LET bodyCols AS List OF Integer = documents.bodyCols
  LET endLines AS List OF Integer = documents.endLines
  MUT values AS List OF json::Json = []
  FOR i = 0 TO len(bodyLines) - 1
    values = collections::append(values, parseDocument(bytes, starts, ends, indents, collections::get(bodyLines, i), collections::get(bodyCols, i), collections::get(endLines, i)))
  NEXT
  RETURN values
END FUNC

DOC
  FUNC expansionLimitCode
  GROUP Reading
  DESC The error code this package raises when a document expands past its
  DESC alias-expansion or total-node budget: 93110001, written `9-311-0001`.
  DESC
  DESC It is the one failure here with no `errorCode::` equivalent -- the
  DESC registry models no "this input expands too far" -- so it takes a code
  DESC from generator 9, the range reserved for codes a package defines for
  DESC itself. Generator-9 codes carry no registry meaning and are not globally
  DESC unique: two unrelated packages may both use this integer for unrelated
  DESC failures. Match it only around a call you already know is this
  DESC package's, never across a broad block.
  RET 93110001.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT yaml
    IMPORT json
    IMPORT io

    FUNC readConfig(text AS String) AS json::Json
      LET value AS json::Json = yaml::parse(text) TRAP(problem)
        IF problem.code = yaml::expansionLimitCode() THEN
          io::printError("that YAML expands too far to read")
          RECOVER json::JsonObj[Map OF String TO json::Json {}]
        END IF
        PROPAGATE
      END TRAP
      RETURN value
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
EXPORT FUNC expansionLimitCode() AS Integer
  RETURN ERR_EXPANSION_LIMIT
END FUNC
